# ECS Design Practices

## Systems Communicate Through Components

Systems must not call into each other. If system A needs to affect system B's behavior, A writes a component — B reads it next frame.

If two systems feel tightly coupled, add an intermediate component (and possibly a new system to populate it).

```cpp
// ❌ BAD — MovementSystem reading raw input
if (InputSystem::isKeyDown(SDLK_RIGHT)) { ... }

// ❌ BAD — InputSystem touching movement fields
vel.x = isRight ? speed : 0.f;

// ✅ GOOD — MoveIntent component decouples input from movement
MoveIntent& intent = MoveIntent::get(ent);
intent.dx = isRight ? 1.f : 0.f;

float dx = MoveIntent::get(ent).dx; // MovementSystem knows nothing about input
```

## Systems Are Cheap, Components Are Not

Prefer more systems over bloated ones. A system per enemy type is fine. A component per enemy type is not — components should be general enough to reuse across entity types. Do not over-split into too many components/systems either; that makes relationships hard to follow.

```cpp
// ❌ BAD — one component per enemy variant
struct BeeAI { ... };
struct TurretAI { ... };

// ✅ OK — one system per variant, shared or minimal components
class BeeSystem final : bagel::NoInstance {};
class TurretSystem final : bagel::NoInstance {};
```

Components are plain-data structs with no behavior — systems operate on them externally.

## Render Layers

Every `Drawable` entity must specify a render layer. Use existing layers in `RenderLayers.h`; add a new layer there if none fit. Do not draw at an implicit z-order.

## Event Polling

Do not poll SDL events outside a dedicated system. Implement an `InputSystem` that polls events and exposes state via a component (e.g. `InputState`). Other systems read that component — they never touch SDL event queues. Avoid global refs (e.g. coffee machine) and global flags (e.g. `isDragging`).

## Global State

`PhysicsContext` and `RenderContext` are known tech debt. Do not add new globals. Pass what a system needs as parameters so behavior is visible from its signature.

## Testing Independent Features

Add a dedicated entry point under `app/` and register it in `app/CMakeLists.txt`. Keep test entry points out of main `main.cpp`; remove them before merging to `main`.

```cmake
# app/CMakeLists.txt — first line for isolated test
set(APP_SOURCES "${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}/my_test.cpp")
```
